WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.919
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book one.

2
00:00:06.219 --> 00:00:09.489
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.789 --> 00:00:17.434
Unit twenty-three: Decisions Under Pressure. Today you will express reasons concisely with participle clauses of reason.

4
00:00:17.734 --> 00:00:20.293
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:21.233 --> 00:00:24.431
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.283 --> 00:00:26.192
Decision.

7
00:00:27.183 --> 00:00:30.228
Decision means a choice made after consideration.

8
00:00:30.817 --> 00:00:33.594
The decision to evacuate saved hundreds of lives.

9
00:00:34.183 --> 00:00:36.929
Your turn. Say: Decision.

10
00:00:39.767 --> 00:00:40.721
Decision.

11
00:00:41.483 --> 00:00:42.345
Pressure.

12
00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:46.336
Pressure means stress caused by urgent demands.

13
00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:49.542
Under pressure, people tend to rely on habit.

14
00:00:50.150 --> 00:00:52.709
Your turn. Say: Pressure.

15
00:00:55.567 --> 00:00:56.440
Pressure.

16
00:00:57.183 --> 00:00:58.266
Uncertainty.

17
00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:02.805
Uncertainty means a state in which outcomes cannot be known.

18
00:01:03.417 --> 00:01:06.194
Leaders must act despite uncertainty.

19
00:01:06.783 --> 00:01:09.482
Your turn. Say: Uncertainty.

20
00:01:12.333 --> 00:01:13.509
Uncertainty.

21
00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:15.158
Risk.

22
00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:19.125
Risk means the possibility of loss or harm.

23
00:01:19.717 --> 00:01:22.541
Every option carried a different kind of risk.

24
00:01:23.150 --> 00:01:25.953
Your turn. Say: Risk.

25
00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:29.673
Risk.

26
00:01:30.417 --> 00:01:31.418
Intuition.

27
00:01:32.417 --> 00:01:35.717
Intuition means understanding reached without conscious reasoning.

28
00:01:36.317 --> 00:01:39.454
The firefighter's intuition told him the floor would collapse.

29
00:01:40.050 --> 00:01:42.726
Your turn. Say: Intuition.

30
00:01:45.567 --> 00:01:46.626
Intuition.

31
00:01:47.383 --> 00:01:48.733
Analysis.

32
00:01:49.717 --> 00:01:53.434
Analysis means detailed, systematic examination.

33
00:01:54.050 --> 00:01:56.491
Careful analysis revealed a hidden pattern.

34
00:01:57.083 --> 00:02:00.049
Your turn. Say: Analysis.

35
00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:04.133
Analysis.

36
00:02:04.883 --> 00:02:06.093
Trade-off.

37
00:02:07.083 --> 00:02:10.952
Trade-off means a compromise between competing benefits.

38
00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:14.618
Speed came at the trade-off of accuracy.

39
00:02:15.217 --> 00:02:18.415
Your turn. Say: Trade-off.

40
00:02:21.267 --> 00:02:22.477
Trade-off.

41
00:02:23.217 --> 00:02:24.322
Consequence.

42
00:02:25.317 --> 00:02:28.860
Consequence means a result of an action or decision.

43
00:02:29.450 --> 00:02:31.902
The consequences of the delay were severe.

44
00:02:32.517 --> 00:02:35.494
Your turn. Say: Consequence.

45
00:02:38.333 --> 00:02:39.729
Consequence.

46
00:02:40.533 --> 00:02:44.196
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:45.050 --> 00:02:54.935
Visal, during the 2011 floods, a provincial governor had to decide whether to evacuate thousands of people with limited information. How do leaders cope?

48
00:02:56.050 --> 00:03:04.772
Facing such uncertainty, the best leaders gather whatever data they can quickly, but they don't wait for perfect information.

49
00:03:05.883 --> 00:03:07.744
And if the data is unclear?

50
00:03:08.850 --> 00:03:18.956
Then experience matters. Having seen similar floods before, an experienced official may sense danger that statistics haven't yet confirmed.

51
00:03:20.050 --> 00:03:22.479
So intuition beats analysis?

52
00:03:23.583 --> 00:03:32.819
Not exactly. Knowing that intuition can be biased, wise leaders test it quickly against facts and adjust as evidence arrives.

53
00:03:33.967 --> 00:03:35.258
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:36.117 --> 00:03:39.266
Grammar focus: Participle clauses of reason.

55
00:03:39.566 --> 00:03:40.787
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:41.483 --> 00:03:44.551
Facing uncertainty, the leader gathered more data.

57
00:03:45.450 --> 00:03:48.622
Having seen similar floods, the official sensed the danger.

58
00:03:49.517 --> 00:03:53.374
Knowing that intuition can be biased, she tested it against facts.

59
00:03:54.333 --> 00:04:05.377
A participle clause can replace 'because/as + clause': Facing uncertainty (= Because he faced uncertainty), he… The implied subject must be the subject of the main clause, or the sentence 'dangles'.

60
00:04:06.117 --> 00:04:10.197
Be careful! Don't say: Facing uncertainty, data was gathered.

61
00:04:12.133 --> 00:04:15.085
Say: Facing uncertainty, she gathered data.

62
00:04:15.385 --> 00:04:17.942
Participle subject = main subject.

63
00:04:18.733 --> 00:04:21.212
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:23.651
Thinking Fast and Slow.

65
00:04:23.951 --> 00:04:30.026
Psychologist Daniel Kahneman distinguished between fast, intuitive thinking and slow, analytical reasoning.

66
00:04:31.033 --> 00:04:38.350
Operating under pressure, people rely heavily on the fast system, which is efficient but vulnerable to biases such as overconfidence.

67
00:04:39.367 --> 00:04:47.775
Gary Klein's research on firefighters, however, showed that experienced experts often make excellent rapid decisions by recognising familiar patterns.

68
00:04:48.767 --> 00:04:57.523
Having studied both perspectives, many researchers conclude that intuition is trustworthy only in environments that provide regular, reliable feedback.

69
00:04:58.533 --> 00:05:05.281
Effective leaders therefore combine quick judgement with deliberate checks, especially when the consequences are irreversible.

70
00:05:06.367 --> 00:05:10.461
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:11.317 --> 00:05:13.444
blank tired, she went to bed early.

72
00:05:13.744 --> 00:05:16.956
A, Feeling. B, Felt. C, To feel.

73
00:05:20.500 --> 00:05:22.105
The answer is A: Feeling.

74
00:05:23.250 --> 00:05:26.260
Understanding reached without conscious reasoning is blank.

75
00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:30.619
A, intuition. B, analysis. C, uncertainty.

76
00:05:34.167 --> 00:05:36.143
The answer is A: intuition.

77
00:05:37.283 --> 00:05:40.119
A result of an action or decision is a blank.

78
00:05:40.419 --> 00:05:44.479
A, risk. B, trade-off. C, consequence.

79
00:05:48.033 --> 00:05:50.648
The answer is C: consequence.

80
00:05:51.783 --> 00:05:53.632
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:53.932 --> 00:06:03.054
A, Facing the storm, the boat was turned back.. B, Facing the storm, the captain turned the boat back.. C, Faced the storm, the captain turned back..

82
00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:11.119
The answer is B: Facing the storm, the captain turned the boat back..

83
00:06:12.283 --> 00:06:16.164
According to the reading, intuition is trustworthy in environments that…

84
00:06:16.464 --> 00:06:22.486
A, provide regular, reliable feedback. B, are completely new. C, have no experts.

85
00:06:26.033 --> 00:06:29.728
The answer is A: provide regular, reliable feedback.

86
00:06:30.933 --> 00:06:34.085
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:34.950 --> 00:06:37.112
When should leaders trust their intuition?

88
00:06:53.917 --> 00:06:55.231
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:55.531 --> 00:07:29.756
Leaders should trust their intuition mainly in situations where they have deep, relevant experience and where the environment provides reliable feedback. Having faced similar problems many times, an expert recognises patterns that cannot easily be explained. However, intuition is dangerous in unfamiliar or complex situations, such as new financial markets, because biases can masquerade as insight. Knowing this, I would trust intuition for urgent decisions but test it against data whenever time allows.

90
00:07:30.056 --> 00:07:31.696
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:32.583 --> 00:07:37.151
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and participle clauses of reason.

92
00:07:37.451 --> 00:07:42.043
Today's value is prudence. Think carefully about risks before you act.

93
00:07:42.343 --> 00:07:45.157
See you in Unit twenty-four. Goodbye!

94
00:07:45.457 --> 00:07:48.295
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

